vscode: click between the brackets, type x, six times
open: release.md

@brief
Six checkboxes, all of them done. Put an `x` in each one.

Nothing to work out. The rep is: land on the space inside the brackets,
replace it, drop a line. The column does not move, so after the first one
you only ever press two keys.

@keys
| `r` | replace the character under the cursor |
| `3l` | three characters right |
| `j` | down a line — keeps the column |

@hints
`2j` gets you off the heading. `3l` from the start of a checkbox line
puts you inside the brackets, and `rx` fills the box.
--
`j` keeps the column you were in, so the next box is directly below the
cursor. Do not re-aim.

@solution
    2j 3l rx  then five times: j rx  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-one keystrokes for six edits, and after the first one your fingers
only know `jrx`.

The thing being drilled is not `r`, it is the pairing of a cheap motion
with a cheap verb. `j` preserving the column is what makes a vertical run
of identical edits nearly free, and vertical runs of identical edits are
most of what you do to config files, tables and checklists.

Chapter 11 turns this into one keystroke per line with `.`, and chapter
21 turns it into one keystroke for the whole file. The hand movement you
are building now is what those chapters compress.

@start release.md
# Release 2.4.0

